1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,540
A defect is more than a mark on a component.

2
00:00:04,540 --> 00:00:06,101
It is a decision.

3
00:00:06,101 --> 00:00:06,835
A delay.

4
00:00:06,835 --> 00:00:11,242
A question your production team needs to answer.

5
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:13,765
Meet ExtendQuality.

6
00:00:13,765 --> 00:00:23,314
A connected inspection workflow that turns visual evidence into traceable decisions, and decisions into batch intelligence, powered by Exasol Personal.

7
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:26,812
Industry feedback sharpened our focus.

8
00:00:26,812 --> 00:00:28,942
A bearing must be tested in motion.

9
00:00:28,942 --> 00:00:37,279
Our next pilot targets static products: molded closures, gaskets, and stamped parts, where visible quality can be meaningfully evaluated.

10
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:42,069
Here is our recorded live-camera demonstration.

11
00:00:42,069 --> 00:00:45,030
The connected phone supplies the frame.

12
00:00:45,030 --> 00:00:51,561
Capture the bearing, run the inspection, and follow the evidence through the pipeline.

13
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:55,635
OpenCV measures image quality.

14
00:00:55,635 --> 00:00:57,637
YOLO supplies visual evidence.

15
00:00:57,637 --> 00:00:59,772
Decision rules route the result.

16
00:00:59,772 --> 00:01:06,310
When evidence is uncertain, a vision language model can support the inspector with an explanation.

17
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:09,471
Human judgment stays in control.

18
00:01:09,471 --> 00:01:13,660
Accept or reject an inspection, and the current disposition updates.

19
00:01:13,660 --> 00:01:16,493
The original model evidence remains available.

20
00:01:16,493 --> 00:01:22,284
Phone captures begin in review, so an unconfirmed image is never silently treated as approved.

21
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:26,301
Exasol Personal is the analytics platform.

22
00:01:26,301 --> 00:01:30,769
Inspection events and review decisions flow into structured tables.

23
00:01:30,769 --> 00:01:36,704
SQL resolves the effective disposition and aggregates results by batch, machine, and day.

24
00:01:36,704 --> 00:01:39,305
A retry queue protects pending records.

25
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:44,236
Now switch to the clearly labeled synthetic demonstration.

26
00:01:44,236 --> 00:01:48,295
Eight hundred and forty records make batch differences visible.

27
00:01:48,295 --> 00:01:55,317
Accepted, rejected, and unresolved cases stay distinct, helping a quality lead see where to investigate next.

28
00:01:56,500 --> 00:01:58,225
The risk engine is explainable.

29
00:01:58,225 --> 00:02:02,344
It uses rejection and unresolved rates, with a minimum evidence threshold.

30
00:02:02,344 --> 00:02:04,069
Daily trends highlight changes.

31
00:02:04,069 --> 00:02:09,301
These are descriptive triage signals, not failure probabilities or remaining life predictions.

32
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:15,720
The opportunity: connect the inspection station to the quality conversation.

33
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:17,643
Which batch needs attention?

34
00:02:17,643 --> 00:02:19,291
Which decisions changed?

35
00:02:19,291 --> 00:02:21,970
What evidence supports the next action?

36
00:02:21,970 --> 00:02:25,266
One connected workflow, with a traceable answer.

37
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:36,091
The next milestone is a manufacturer pilot: select one static product, agree defect definitions, collect representative images, and validate performance against expert inspection.

38
00:02:36,091 --> 00:02:40,270
Calibrated dimensional measurement and automatic rejection remain future work.

39
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:45,290
Built for the Exasol A I and Data Challenge, twenty twenty six.

40
00:02:45,290 --> 00:02:48,718
Explore the public repository and reproducible run guide.

41
00:02:48,718 --> 00:02:49,621
ExtendQuality.

42
00:02:49,621 --> 00:02:52,689
From a captured image, to a decision you can trace.

43
00:02:52,689 --> 00:02:54,313
Let's build the next pilot.